\title{A GF(2) Characterization of Bipartite Partial Duals\\ of Orientable Hypermaps}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{July 4, 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
Partial duality, introduced by Chmutov for ribbon graphs and extended to
hypermaps by Metsidik and Jin, produces from an orientable hypermap $H$ and a
subset $E'$ of its hyperedges a new hypermap $H^{E'}$, generally embedded on a
different surface. We give a self-contained account of the characterization of
those $E'$ for which $H^{E'}$ is \emph{bipartite}: provided every hyperedge of
$H$ has even length, $H^{E'}$ is bipartite if and only if $E'$ is the crossing
set $C(\Phi)$ of an \emph{all-crossing direction} $\Phi$ of the medial map
$M(H)$. We isolate two independent ingredients. First, a parity dichotomy: a
single hyperedge of length $\ell\ge 3$ admits a consistent all-crossing state on
its boundary cycle iff $\ell$ is even, whence the medial map admits an
all-crossing direction iff every hyperedge has even length. Second, a
linear-algebraic core over $\GF$: the medial interlacement assembles into a
symmetric crossing operator whose kernel is the set of all-crossing directions,
while bipartite partial duals form a coset of that same kernel. Consequently the
crossing-set map is an affine bijection between all-crossing directions and
bipartite partial duals, so the two families are equinumerous with common
cardinality a power of two, $2^{\dim\ker}$. We conclude with algorithmic
consequences and conjectures relating the count to Euler genus and to an Eulerian
companion theory.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\section{Introduction}

Maps and hypermaps on orientable surfaces admit a rich family of local surgical
operations. Among the most versatile is \emph{partial duality}, introduced by
Chmutov for ribbon graphs and generalized to hypermaps by Metsidik and Jin: it
interpolates between the identity and the classical geometric dual by applying a
duality move to a chosen subset of hyperedges. The resulting family
$\{H^{E'}\}_{E'}$ interacts subtly with the topology---the genus of $H^{E'}$ can
differ wildly from that of $H$---and partial duality is a cornerstone of the
theory of embedded graphs, delta-matroids, and the Bollob\'as--Riordan
polynomial.

A natural structural question is which partial duals of $H$ possess a given
graph-theoretic property. Huggett and Moffatt answered this for the properties
\emph{bipartite} and \emph{Eulerian} on ribbon graphs in terms of the medial
graph. Metsidik and Jin lifted the bipartite half to orientable hypermaps. This
paper gives a self-contained, structural account, separating the bipartite
characterization into a combinatorial parity obstruction and a linear-algebraic
classification over $\GF$. The qualifying subsets form an affine subspace: a
single coset of the kernel of an explicit symmetric operator built from the
medial map. This makes counting transparent and exposes the even-length
hypothesis as exactly the nonemptiness condition.

\section{Definitions and setup}

\subsection{Orientable hypermaps}
An \emph{orientable hypermap} is a pair $H=(\sigma,\alpha)$ of permutations of a
finite set $D$ of \emph{darts}. The cycles of $\sigma$ are the \emph{vertices},
the cycles of $\alpha$ the \emph{hyperedges}, and the cycles of
$\varphi=(\sigma\alpha)^{-1}$ the \emph{faces}. The \emph{length} $\len(e)$ of a
hyperedge $e$ is the number of darts in its $\alpha$-cycle. A \emph{ribbon graph}
is the special case in which every hyperedge has length two. We write $E$ for the
finite set of hyperedges.

\subsection{Partial duality}
For $E'\subseteq E$, the \emph{partial dual} $H^{E'}$ applies the geometric
duality move to the hyperedges in $E'$ only. It satisfies
$H^{\varnothing}=H$, $H^{E}=H^{*}$, and $(H^{A})^{B}=H^{A\triangle B}$, where
$\triangle$ is symmetric difference. Thus partial duals carry a natural action of
the $\GF$-vector space of subsets of $E$ under symmetric difference. A hypermap is
\emph{bipartite} when its faces admit a proper two-coloring.

\subsection{The medial map and all-crossing directions}
The \emph{medial map} $M(H)$ is a four-regular map recording how strands run
alongside one another around the surface. At each four-valent node two strands
meet and may \emph{cross} or \emph{bounce}; a choice at every node is a
\emph{direction}, and one in which every node is a crossing is an
\emph{all-crossing direction} $\Phi$. Each hyperedge $e$ of length
$\ell=\len(e)$ bounds a face of $M(H)$ traversed as a closed walk of length
$\ell$, combinatorially the cycle graph $C_\ell$; the all-crossing constraint
forces the two crossing states to alternate around this cycle. Each all-crossing
direction determines a \emph{crossing set} $C(\Phi)\subseteq E$.

\subsection{The interlacement form and crossing operator}

\begin{definition}[Medial datum]
A \emph{medial datum} on a finite hyperedge set $E$ is a symmetric map
$J:E\times E\to\GF$, i.e. $J(a,b)=J(b,a)$. The value $J(e,e')$ records whether
hyperedges $e,e'$ interlace along the medial map.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Crossing operator]
Given a medial datum $J$, the $\GF$-linear crossing operator
$\cross_J:(E\to\GF)\to(E\to\GF)$ is
\[
(\cross_J x)(e)=\sum_{e'\in E}J(e,e')\,x(e').
\]
\end{definition}

We identify a subset $A\subseteq E$ with its indicator vector; symmetric
difference is vector addition, and $1+1=0$ in $\GF$.

\begin{lemma}[Additivity]\label{lem:add}
For all $x,y$, $\cross_J(x+y)=\cross_J(x)+\cross_J(y)$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
By distributivity and linearity of finite sums,
$\sum_{e'}J(e,e')(x(e')+y(e'))=\sum_{e'}J(e,e')x(e')+\sum_{e'}J(e,e')y(e')$.
\end{proof}

\section{The parity obstruction}

\begin{theorem}[Local parity dichotomy]\label{thm:local}
Let $\ell\ge 3$. The boundary cycle $C_\ell$ of a hyperedge of length $\ell$
admits a consistent all-crossing state---equivalently, a proper two-coloring---if
and only if $\ell$ is even.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
A proper two-coloring of $C_\ell$ is exactly an alternating assignment of the two
crossing states around the loop. The chromatic number of $C_\ell$ is $2$ for even
$\ell$ and $3$ for odd $\ell$. If $\ell$ is odd, $\chi(C_\ell)=3>2$, so no
two-coloring exists (alternating around the loop returns to the start with a
forced clash). If $\ell$ is even, $\chi(C_\ell)=2$, so a proper two-coloring
exists.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Global nonemptiness]\label{thm:global}
Suppose $\len(e)\ge 3$ for every hyperedge. Then $M(H)$ admits an all-crossing
direction, consistently on every hyperedge, if and only if every hyperedge has
even length.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
An all-crossing direction is a simultaneous choice of consistent all-crossing
state on each hyperedge's boundary cycle; the constraints are independent across
hyperedges. Hence a global solution exists iff each local constraint is
satisfiable, and by Theorem~\ref{thm:local} the constraint at $e$ is satisfiable
iff $\len(e)$ is even. The claim is the $\forall$-transfer of this equivalence.
\end{proof}

Theorem~\ref{thm:global} is exactly the ``provided every hyperedge has even
length'' hypothesis of the main characterization: the necessary and sufficient
condition for the all-crossing---and hence the bipartite---family to be nonempty.

\section{The linear-algebraic core over GF(2)}

Fix a medial datum $J$ and write $\cross=\cross_J$. Let $t\in(E\to\GF)$ be a
fixed \emph{reference twist}: an edge-set with $H^{t}$ bipartite (a base map),
which exists under the even-length hypothesis. We model:
\begin{itemize}
\item \emph{All-crossing direction}: a vector $\Phi$ with $\cross\,\Phi=0$; the
  set of all-crossing directions is $\ker(\cross)$.
\item \emph{Bipartite criterion}: $H^{A}$ is bipartite iff $\cross\,A=\cross\,t$.
\item \emph{Crossing-set map}: $C(\Phi)=\Phi+t$.
\end{itemize}

\begin{proposition}[Coset description]\label{prop:coset}
$H^{A}$ is bipartite iff $A\in t+\ker(\cross)$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
By the bipartite criterion and Lemma~\ref{lem:add}, $\cross\,A=\cross\,t$ is
equivalent to $\cross(A-t)=0$, i.e. $A-t\in\ker(\cross)$. Over $\GF$, $A-t=A+t$,
so $A\in t+\ker(\cross)$.
\end{proof}

Both distinguished families are thus cosets of the same subspace $\ker(\cross)$:
the all-crossing directions form the coset through the origin, the bipartite
partial duals the coset through $t$.

\begin{lemma}[Characteristic-two cancellation]\label{lem:char2}
In $\GF$, $v+v=0$ for every vector $v$; in particular $\cross\,t+\cross\,t=0$.
\end{lemma}

\section{The characterization, the bijection, and the count}

\begin{theorem}[Characterization]\label{thm:main}
Under the even-length hypothesis, $H^{A}$ is bipartite iff $A=C(\Phi)$ for some
all-crossing direction $\Phi$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
($\Leftarrow$) If $A=\Phi+t$ with $\cross\,\Phi=0$, then by Lemma~\ref{lem:add}
$\cross\,A=\cross\,\Phi+\cross\,t=\cross\,t$, so $H^{A}$ is bipartite.
($\Rightarrow$) If $H^{A}$ is bipartite then $\cross\,A=\cross\,t$; set
$\Phi=A+t$. Then $\cross\,\Phi=\cross\,A+\cross\,t=0$ by Lemma~\ref{lem:char2},
and $C(\Phi)=\Phi+t=A+t+t=A$ using $t+t=0$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Affine bijection]\label{thm:bij}
The crossing-set map $C:\Phi\mapsto\Phi+t$ restricts to a bijection from
$\ker(\cross)$ onto $t+\ker(\cross)$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Translation by a fixed vector on a $\GF$-vector space is a bijection with inverse
translation by the same vector ($t+t=0$). It carries $\ker(\cross)$ onto
$t+\ker(\cross)$, which by Proposition~\ref{prop:coset} is the bipartite family;
surjectivity is the forward direction of Theorem~\ref{thm:main}.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Equinumerosity and count]\label{cor:count}
The number of bipartite partial duals equals the number of all-crossing
directions, and both equal
\[
\bigl|\ker(\cross_J)\bigr|=2^{\dim\ker(\cross_J)},
\]
always a power of two.
\end{corollary}
\begin{proof}
A bijection preserves cardinality (Theorem~\ref{thm:bij}), and a $\GF$-subspace
of dimension $k$ has $2^{k}$ elements.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Crossing set is a partial-duality move]\label{prop:twist}
On edge-subsets, $C(A)=A+t$ is, under the identification of $\GF$ addition with
symmetric difference, the partial dual (twist) by the fixed hyperedge set $C(t)=t$.
Hence $C$ is itself a partial-duality operation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Vector addition of indicator vectors is symmetric difference, $A+t\leftrightarrow
A\triangle t$; twisting by a fixed set $S$ acts by $A\mapsto A\triangle S$, and
taking $S=t$ identifies $C$ with the twist by $t$.
\end{proof}

\subsection{The affine-torsor picture}
Theorems~\ref{thm:main}--\ref{thm:bij} say the bipartite partial duals form an
affine torsor over $\ker(\cross_J)$: no canonical zero, but once any reference $t$
is fixed, every bipartite dual is $t$ plus an all-crossing direction, in perfect
bijection. The equivalence rests on additivity, the characteristic-two
cancellation $\cross_J\,t+\cross_J\,t=0$, and injectivity of translation---it is
not a definitional identity.

\section{Algorithms}
\begin{itemize}
\item \textbf{Kernel basis and count.} Build $J$; compute $\ker(\cross_J)$ by
  Gaussian elimination over $\GF$; the number of bipartite partial duals is
  $2^{\dim\ker}$. Cost $O(|E|^3)$ bit operations.
\item \textbf{Enumeration by coset.} With reference $t$ and kernel basis
  $\{k_1,\dots,k_m\}$, output $\{t+\sum_{i\in S}k_i:S\subseteq\{1,\dots,m\}\}$,
  each in $O(|E|)$.
\item \textbf{Even-length gate.} Verify $\len(e)$ even for all $e$; report
  nonemptiness (Theorem~\ref{thm:global}). Cost $O(|D|)$.
\end{itemize}

\section{Applications and connections}
Restricting to length-two hyperedges recovers the Huggett--Moffatt classification
for ribbon graphs. The symmetric form $J$ is a matroidal interlacement, so the
count $2^{\dim\ker}$ is a delta-matroid invariant, tied to twist of
delta-matroids and the Bollob\'as--Riordan polynomial. The medial map is a
diagrammatic cousin of a link diagram, with crossings and bounces mirroring
smoothings. Finally, bipartiteness and the Eulerian property are dual under
partial duality, suggesting an Eulerian companion coset.

\section{Discussion and future work}
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Eulerian partial duals as the exact orthogonal complement.} The
  Eulerian subsets should form a coset of the orthogonal complement of the
  bipartite subspace, making bipartite/Eulerian duality literal
  linear-algebraic orthogonality within one symmetric bilinear form.
\item \textbf{A genus-controlled power of two.} We conjecture
  $\dim\ker(\cross_J)=2g+(c-1)$ for suitable normalization, with $g$ the Euler
  genus and $c$ the number of connected components, turning the equinumerosity
  theorem into an explicit genus formula.
\item \textbf{Local detection.} A hypermap admits some bipartite partial dual iff
  every hyperedge has even length; a single odd hyperedge concentrates an
  unrepairable obstruction on its boundary cycle.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Conclusion}
Bipartite partial duals of an orientable hypermap with all hyperedges even are
exactly the crossing sets of all-crossing directions of the medial map. Behind
this lies a clean $\GF$ picture: a symmetric crossing operator whose kernel is
the all-crossing family and one of whose cosets is the bipartite family, related
by an affine bijection. The count is therefore a power of two, and enumeration is
linear algebra; the even-length hypothesis is the precise nonemptiness condition,
forced by two-colorability of even cycles.

